From: Audio speakers <audio-speakers@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH candidate] ASoC: qcom: keep the guard boot parameters visible to the module loader

The guard is built as a module (CONFIG_SND_SOC_SP11_GUARD=m). With
MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX set to "sp11." the stored parameter names became
"sp11.allow_nominal" and "sp11.nospk"; the module loader splits
"sp11.allow_nominal=1" at the dot, takes "sp11" as the module name and looks
for the bare name, so the boot parameters were ignored. The device boot of
2026-11-05 logged "sp11: unknown parameter 'allow_nominal' ignored" and the
guard snapshot reported allow_nominal: 0, which is why the nominal calibration
could never unmute. The kill switch sp11.nospk was silently ineffective for
the same reason.

Use an empty prefix for the module build and keep "sp11." for built-in builds.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
---
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sp11-guard.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sp11-guard.c
@@ -30,8 +30,20 @@
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
 #include <sound/sp11-guard.h>
 
+/*
+ * This file is a module (CONFIG_SND_SOC_SP11_GUARD=m). The module loader splits
+ * "sp11.allow_nominal=1" at the dot, matches "sp11" as the module name and looks
+ * for the bare parameter name; a non-empty prefix here made the stored name
+ * "sp11.allow_nominal" and the loader ignored the boot parameter ("sp11: unknown
+ * parameter 'allow_nominal' ignored", device boot 2026-11-05). Built-in builds
+ * keep the prefix so "sp11.nospk" still works on the kernel command line.
+ */
 #undef MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX
+#ifdef MODULE
+#define MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX ""
+#else
 #define MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX "sp11."
+#endif
 
 static bool nospk;
 module_param(nospk, bool, 0444);
